of \code{blocks.R}, and puts it back into \code{manifest$source_md5} when one
fails, also when this rule is \code{warn} or \code{off}.

From the set \code{house_2026_11_26}, the rule also fails each of these:
\itemize{
\item an element of \code{blocks} that is not a call \code{pbn::block(fn, format, items)}.
\item a \code{format} that is not one string, or that names no house formatter
and no function of \code{formatters.R}.
\item an \code{items} that is not a literal \code{c("<id>:<item>", ...)} of strings.
\item a top-level expression of \code{vNN_functions/formatters.R} that does not
assign a name to \verb{function(...)}.
\item a name of \code{formatters.R} that a file other than \code{blocks.R} uses or
assigns.
}

The rule does not run the blocks, so it does not see their raw values.
\code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}}, \code{\link[=update_blocks]{update_blocks()}}, \code{\link[=show_blocks]{show_blocks()}}, \code{\link[=show_block]{show_block()}} and
\code{\link[=txt]{txt()}} check each raw value as \code{\link[=block]{block()}} says.
\item \code{real_results_are_fake} (WARN): a file \code{vNN_results/real/results.qs2}
holds \code{manifest$fake_real_data} \code{TRUE}. Its run had
\code{fake_real_data = TRUE} of \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}}, so its numbers are
not those of the real data. It passes when no such file is in the
list of files. In a git repository, a real results file that git
ignores is not in the list, so the rule does not see it.
\item \code{guidelines_valid} (FAIL): a file \verb{guidelines/<x>.yml} breaks the
checklist schema below. Each such file gives one FAIL, and its detail
names its first 3 problems and counts the rest. The rule reads each
\code{.yml} file directly in \verb{guidelines/}. It does not read a file in a
folder under \verb{guidelines/}, or a file with another extension, such as
\code{.yaml}.
\item \code{guidelines_covered} (WARN): a result item of a valid file
\verb{guidelines/<x>.yml} has no block, or a block names an item that no
valid file has. Each such item and each such tag gives one WARN. The
rule reads the literal \code{items} of each \code{\link[=block]{block()}} in the \code{blocks.R} of
the newest functions folder, without running code. A tag of a file
that fails \code{guidelines_valid} gives no WARN. \code{\link[=make_checklists]{make_checklists()}} then
checks the manuscript text against the formatted strings of the blocks.
\item \code{display_numbering} (FAIL): the numbered figures and tables of the
outputs, of the manuscript and of its citations do not agree. The rule
reads the output folders \verb{vNN_results/<scenario>/output/} and
\verb{vNN_results/<scenario>/output_fake_data/} of the newest run version.
It reads each name there without the prefix \code{fake_data_}. Each name
MUST be one of these:
\itemize{
\item \verb{fig_N_<name>} or \verb{fig_sN_<name>}, a figure, with any extension. \code{N}
is a whole number from 1, and \code{s} marks a supplementary figure.
\item \code{tables.xlsx}, with one sheet \code{tab_N} or \code{tab_sN} for each numbered
table.
\item \code{data_behind_figures.xlsx}, with the plotted data in sheets
\verb{fig_N_<name>} or \verb{fig_sN_<name>}. A figure MAY have more than one
sheet.
\item \verb{fig_N_<name>_external.txt}, \verb{fig_sN_<name>_external.txt},
\verb{tab_N_<name>_external.txt} or \verb{tab_sN_<name>_external.txt}, an item
that the output function does not make, such as a flow chart.
\item \verb{nonum_<name>}, anything else.
}

A sheet \verb{nonum_<name>} passes in both workbooks. A file \verb{tab_N_<name>}
that does not end with \verb{_external.txt} fails, because tables go in
\code{tables.xlsx}. Each figure file other than an \verb{_external.txt} item MUST
have a sheet of its number in \code{data_behind_figures.xlsx} of its
folder. Each sheet \code{fig_N_} MUST have a figure file of its number there.

The manuscript is the newest version \code{NN} of
\verb{manuscript/manual/vNN_<INITIALS>.docx}, then its \verb{_si} files, and the
files \verb{manuscript/built/vNN/*.md}. Without a manuscript, the rule checks
the names only. A citation is "Figure 1", "Fig. 1", "Table 1", "Figure
S1" or "Supplementary Figure 1", which is Figure S1. A range such as
"Tables 1-3" cites each number of it. A list needs the plural word, such
as "Figures 1 and 2". A number after a comma needs another separator, a
closing mark or the end of the text after it. So "Figures 1 and 2, 25
persons" cites Figures 1 and 2 only. The text of a tracked deletion and
of the old place of moved text in a \code{.docx} file does not count. There
are four series: the figures, the tables, the supplementary figures and
the supplementary tables. In each series, the first citations MUST come
in the order 1, 2, 3, with no gap. A paragraph that starts with "Figure
2." or "Table S1:" is a caption. So is "\strong{Figure 2.}" in Markdown
emphasis, and "\figure{fig.png}", the text of an image. The
text MUST cite each captioned item outside its own caption.

The numbered items of the outputs are the figure files, the sheets
\code{tab_} of \code{tables.xlsx} and the \verb{_external.txt} items of
\verb{vNN_results/real/output/}. Without that folder, they are the items of
the first \verb{output_fake_data/} folder of the version in name order, such
as \verb{fake/output_fake_data/} of a one-file project. Each numbered item
MUST be cited or captioned in the manuscript. Each item of the
manuscript MUST be a numbered item of the outputs. The PASS line names
the folder.
\item \code{unknown_path} (WARN): the house layout does not name a top-level file or
folder. The layout names \code{vNN_run.R}, \verb{vNN_functions/}, \verb{vNN_results/},
\verb{vNN_cache_gitexclude/}, \verb{vNN_test/}, \code{vNN_check_functions.R},
\code{vNN_one_file.R}, \code{check_project.R} and \code{pbn.yml}, among others. From
the set \code{house_2026_11_19}, it also names \code{vNN_frozen.md5} of
\code{\link[=freeze_version]{freeze_version()}}. From the set \code{house_2026_11_26}, it also names the
folder \verb{guidelines/}. A top-level dotfile and a top-level
\verb{*.Rproj} file are never unknown. The rule also reads the files
directly in \verb{vNN_results/}. There it names the run log \code{run.md}, its PDF
\code{run.pdf} and its errors file \code{run_errors_gitexclude.md}, and no rule
needs them. It reads no deeper, so it never
warns on the folders \verb{output/} and \verb{output_fake_data/} that
\code{\link[=make_outputs]{make_outputs()}} writes in \verb{vNN_results/<scenario>/}, or on the folder
\verb{sims_gitexclude/} that \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} writes there.
}

A checklist file \verb{guidelines/<x>.yml} holds one reporting guideline, in
the checklist schema of the methods repository. Copy the file from there.
\code{guidelines_valid} fails the file unless each of these is true:
\itemize{
\item The file is valid YAML, and a map.
\item It has no key other than \code{id}, \code{title}, \code{version}, \code{source},
\code{citation}, \code{designs}, \code{design} and \code{items}.
\item \code{id} and \code{title} are each one string. \code{id} has only the characters
\code{a-z}, \code{0-9} and \code{-}, and no other file has the same \code{id}.
\item \code{version}, \code{source} and \code{citation} MAY be missing. Each one that is
there is one string.
\item A key that is there has a value. \verb{version:} with nothing after it, or
\verb{citation: ~}, fails. This holds for \code{designs} and \code{design} too, and
for the \code{designs} of an entry.
\item \code{designs} MAY be missing. When it is there, it is a YAML sequence of
strings, such as \verb{[cohort]}. One string, such as \code{designs: cohort}, or a
map fails. The same holds for the \code{designs} of an entry.
\item \code{design} MAY be missing. When it is there, it is one of \code{designs}.
\item \code{items} is a YAML sequence with at least one entry. A map fails.
\item Each entry has no key other than \code{item}, \code{section}, \code{topic}, \code{kind},
\code{designs} and \code{text}. Its \code{item}, \code{section}, \code{topic}, \code{kind} and \code{text}
are each one string. Quote an item id such as \code{"2"}, because YAML reads
\code{2} as a number.
\item The \code{kind} of each entry is \code{result}, \code{prose} or \code{author}.
\item The \code{designs} of an entry MAY be missing, and the entry then belongs to
each design of the file. When it is there, the file has \code{designs}, and
each value is one of them.
\item An item id comes once per design, among the entries that \code{design}
keeps. STROBE has three entries with the item id \code{15}, one for each
design, so it passes.
}

A project copy MAY add the key \code{design}, such as \code{design: cohort}. pbn
then keeps the entries whose \code{designs} include it, and the entries
without \code{designs}.

The R parser, not a regular expression, finds the calls, the assignments
and the symbols.

\code{vNN_one_file.R} at the top level is the file of \code{\link[=to_one_file]{to_one_file()}}. It holds
the paths of a server by design, and pbn writes it. So \code{loc_per_file},
\code{cyclomatic_complexity}, \code{no_local_assign}, \code{no_absolute_paths},
\code{no_plot_title}, \code{no_plot_subtitle} and \code{no_plot_caption} do not read it,
and no rule takes it for a run script. Put it in git: it is what
ran on the server.
}
\section{The file pbn.yml}{

Each project MUST have the file \code{pbn.yml} in its top folder \code{path}. pbn
reads no other file. A file \code{pbn.yaml} stops \code{check_project()} with the
advice to rename it. The smallest \code{pbn.yml} is one line:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{extends: house_2026_11_25
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The key \code{extends} names a house rule set. A set is a dated file in the
folder \verb{rules/} of pbn, and pbn never changes a set after its release. A
change to the house rules is a new set with a new date. pbn never chooses
a set for a project. So a new version of pbn does not change the rules of
a project. To read the set, run
\code{readLines(system.file("rules", "house_2026_11_25.yml", package = "pbn"))}.

The set gives each rule one of three levels:
\itemize{
\item \code{fail}: a finding is FAIL, and \code{check_project.R} ends with status 1.
\item \code{warn}: a finding is WARN.
\item \code{off}: the rule does not run, and its one line is OFF.
}

The parameters of the rules are \code{limit} of \code{loc_per_file}, \code{limit} of
\code{cyclomatic_complexity}, \code{limit} of \code{comment_share} and \code{forbid} of
\code{no_local_assign}. The list of the rules above gives their house values.

The key \code{rules} MAY change the level of a rule, a parameter, or both. A
change merges into the entry of the set. So \code{cyclomatic_complexity: {limit: 25}} keeps the level \code{fail}. The short form \code{lower_case_names: warn} changes the level only. \code{forbid} MAY list fewer constructs than the
house set.

The key \code{allow} MAY list paths for each rule, relative to \code{path}. A path
allows the findings with that path and the findings of each path under
it. So the folder \code{adhoc} allows \code{adhoc/Notes.txt} and
\code{adhoc/Old/Data.csv}, but not \code{adhoc2/x.R}. A final \code{/} changes nothing:
\code{adhoc} and \verb{adhoc/} are the same entry. pbn does not look on the disk
for a file or a folder of that name. An allowed finding is WARN, with
\code{(allowed)} before its fix. A path that matches no finding is a WARN of the
rule \code{allow}.

Three rules are locked: \code{results_contract}, \code{gitexclude_not_tracked} and
\code{fake_data_names}. The first two keep data for each person out of git. The
third keeps the output of fake data apart from the output of real data.
\code{pbn.yml} cannot set a locked rule to \code{warn} or \code{off}, and cannot allow a
path for it. It cannot name a rule of \code{\link[=check_results]{check_results()}} either.

The key \code{ignore} MAY list folders at the top level, such as \verb{archive/},
that no rule reads: their files give no finding, not even a WARN. Use it
for old work that nothing runs. A final \code{/} changes nothing. An entry
MUST be one folder at the top level that the house layout of the set does
not name, so it cannot be \verb{manuscript/}, \verb{background/}, \verb{adhoc/} or a
folder of a version. From the set \code{house_2026_11_26}, it cannot be
\verb{guidelines/} either. A folder that does not exist is no error. The locked rules still
read an ignored folder, so a tracked file with \code{gitexclude} in its name
fails there too. The first line of the report counts the ignored
folders.

This \code{pbn.yml} changes two rules, allows three paths and ignores one
folder:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{extends: house_2026_11_25
rules:
  cyclomatic_complexity: \{limit: 25\}
  unknown_path: off
allow:
  lower_case_names: [adhoc/Old_notes.txt, adhoc/Drafts/]
  no_local_assign:
    - adhoc/explore.R
ignore: [archive/]
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{check_project()} and \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}} stop before any rule runs on
each of these, with a message that names the key:
\itemize{
\item there is no \code{pbn.yml}. The message gives the one line to write.
\item there is a file \code{pbn.yaml}, or a file \code{pbn_allow.txt}. For
\code{pbn_allow.txt}, the message gives its lines as the YAML of the key
\code{allow}.
\item \code{pbn.yml} is not valid YAML. This includes a key that is there twice and
a tab. The message holds the message of the yaml package.
\item there is no key \code{extends}, or it names a set that pbn does not have.
\item a key other than \code{extends}, \code{rules}, \code{allow} and \code{ignore}.
\item a name that is not a rule. The message names the nearest rule.
\item a rule that the set of \code{extends} does not have. The message names the
oldest set with it.
\item a parameter that the rule does not have.
\item a level other than \code{fail}, \code{warn} and \code{off}.
\item a \code{limit} that is not a whole number of 1 or more. For
\code{comment_share}, a \code{limit} that is not a number from 0 to 1.
\item a construct in \code{forbid} that the rule does not know, or an empty
\code{forbid}. To turn the rule off, set its level to \code{off}.
\item a value under \code{allow} that is not a path, for example \code{off}. YAML reads
\code{off}, \code{yes} and \code{no} as booleans, so a path with such a name needs
quotes.
\item a change or an allowed path of a locked rule.
\item an entry of \code{ignore} that is not one folder at the top level, is a name
of the house layout, is there twice, or is a file.
}
}

\examples{
home <- file.path(tempdir(), "pbn_check_example")
dir.create(file.path(home, "v01_functions"), recursive = TRUE)
writeLines("extends: house_2026_11_25", file.path(home, "pbn.yml"))
writeLines(
  'pbn::initialize_project(version = "v01", home = ".", folders = c(main = "x"))',
  file.path(home, "v01_run.R")
)
writeLines("# A fake project", file.path(home, "README.md"))
writeLines(c("# Changelog", "", "## v01"), file.path(home, "CHANGELOG.md"))
pbn::check_project(home)

unlink(home, recursive = TRUE)
}
